RECHECK for TASK-20260916-9da6e0 -- hand-check, NOT a measurement.
Exact arithmetic in F_p[X_1..X_N]; no sampling, no timing, no runs.

==========================================================================
CHECK 1  Example 1 of Nagao 2015/984 (PDF p.5) -- internal consistency
==========================================================================
printed line 1  F := (X^2+X)(Y^2+Y) + (X^2+X)(Y^2+Z)   = XY + XZ + X^2Y + X^2Z   deg 3
printed expansion  X^2Y+Y^2Z+YZ+X^2Z+XY^2+XZ           = XZ + YZ + X^2Y + X^2Z + XY^2 + Y^2Z   deg 3
printed middle line (three copies of (X^2+X)(Y^2+Y))   = XY + XZ + X^2Y + X^2Z   deg 3
printed final line (X+Z)(Y^2+Y) + (X^2+X)(Y+Z)         = XZ + YZ + X^2Y + X^2Z + XY^2 + Y^2Z   deg 3

line1 == printed expansion ? False
line1 == middle line       ? True
line1 == final line        ? False
printed expansion == final ? True
=> the printed chain is INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT: its first line and
   its last line are different polynomials, differing by XY + YZ + XY^2 + Y^2Z

reconstruction  F := (X^2+Z)(Y^2+Y) + (X^2+X)(Y^2+Z)   = XZ + YZ + X^2Y + X^2Z + XY^2 + Y^2Z   deg 3
reconstruction == printed expansion ? True
reconstruction == printed final     ? True
reconstruction ==  0 mod S_fe       ? True
printed line 1 ==  0 mod S_fe       ? True

why it matters for the lemma being illustrated:
  the reconstruction's multipliers are (X^2+Z) on (Y^2+Y) and
  (Y^2+Z) on (X^2+X), both of degree 2 > deg F - p = 1, so it IS an
  instance the lemma has to repair; the final line's multipliers
  (X+Z) and (Y+Z) have degree 1 = deg F - p, which is the lemma's
  conclusion.  Line 1 AS PRINTED collapses to (X^2+X)(Y+Z), whose
  multiplier already has degree 1, so as printed the example
  illustrates nothing.
  line1 as printed == (X^2+X)(Y+Z) ? True

==========================================================================
CHECK 2  inductive step of Nagao 2013/549 Lemma 2 (= Lemma 3 of 2015/984)
==========================================================================
2a. identity (3):  (B^p-B)G == (B^p-B)(G - LT(G) + LT(G)/A^{p-1})
                              + (LT(G)/A^p)(A^p-A)(B^p-B)
   p=2  G = 1 + Y + X^2Y            LT(G) = X^2Y        identity holds ? True
   p=3  G = 1 + Y + X^3Y            LT(G) = X^3Y        identity holds ? True

2b. full descent, checking at every step: G^new gives the same F,
    psi strictly decreases, and the terminal tuple meets the bound
    deg G'_i <= deg F - p.
   p=2 N=2  F = X^2Y + XY^2                  deg F = 3  steps = 1
            start  ['Y^2', 'X^2']
            end    ['Y', 'X']   max deg = 1   bound deg F - p = 1   OK ? True
            terminated because NUM(G) = 1        [the paper's first branch]
   p=2 N=2  F = X^2 + Y^2 + X^3 + X^2Y + XY^2 + Y^3 deg F = 3  steps = 1
            start  ['X + Y^2', 'Y + X^2']
            end    ['X + Y', 'X + Y']   max deg = 1   bound deg F - p = 1   OK ? True
            terminated because NUM(G) = 1        [the paper's first branch]
   p=3 N=2  F = 2X^3Y + 2XY^3 + 2X^3Y^3      deg F = 6  steps = 0
            start  ['Y^3', 'X^3']
            end    ['Y^3', 'X^3']   max deg = 3   bound deg F - p = 3   OK ? True
            terminated because NUM(G) = 2 and deg psi(G) = 6 = D   [THE CASE THE PAPER'S WRITE-UP OMITS]
   p=2 N=3  F = X^2Y^2Z + X^2YZ^2 + XY^2Z^2 + X^2Y^2Z^2 deg F = 6  steps = 0
            start  ['Y^2Z^2', 'X^2Z^2', 'X^2Y^2']
            end    ['Y^2Z^2', 'X^2Z^2', 'X^2Y^2']   max deg = 4   bound deg F - p = 4   OK ? True
            terminated because NUM(G) = 3 and deg psi(G) = 6 = D   [THE CASE THE PAPER'S WRITE-UP OMITS]

2d. claim 1) of the proof is printed as  X_{I_1}^p | G_{I_i}  (the whole
    multiplier).  What the argument establishes, and all identity (3)
    needs, is  X_{I_1}^p | LT(G_{I_i}).  Witness that the printed form
    is strictly stronger and FALSE:
    p=2 N=2, G = (X + Y^2, Y + X^2);  psi = (2, 2), IND = [0, 1]
    X_{I_1}^p = X^2 divides the whole G_{I_2} = Y + X^2 ? False
    X_{I_1}^p = X^2 divides LT(G_{I_2}) = X^2 ? True
    -> printed claim 1) fails on this tuple; the LT form holds, and
       the step goes through on the LT form (2b ran this tuple).

2c. where the GRADED order is load-bearing (errata-extraction-20260921):
    the NUM(G)=1 branch concludes D = deg F = deg psi(G).  That step
    needs deg psi(G) >= deg G_i + p for every i, which is what a
    graded order gives and a merely lexicographic one does not.
    witness p=2 N=2, G=(Y^5, 0): psi = (2, 5), total degree 7, deg F = 7,
    NUM = 1, so deg G_1 = 5 <= deg F - p = 5 ? True

